    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe method follows the surrounding `@Named` pattern correctly (javadoc style, null-guard, mapping annotation placement all consistent), so at a glance it fits. But there's a real structural mismatch with this codebase's own convention.\n\n**The Luhn computation belongs in a `util` class, not inline in the mapper interface.**\n\nThis mapper has two distinct kinds of `@Named` method:\n\n1. **Trivial field formatting**, kept inline: `formatDisplayName` (concatenation), `formatInitials` (one helper call), `formatMembershipNumber` (`String.format`), `formatMembershipLevel` (a couple of `if`s), `formatContactPreference` (a ternary). These read Owner fields and do light shaping.\n2. **Self-contained algorithms**, extracted to `util/` and reached via a one-line delegating method: `normalizeAddress` \u2192 `AddressNormalizer.normalize`, `normalizeTelephone` \u2192 `TelephoneNormalizer.toE164`, and `formatLocality` \u2192 `LocalityResolver.resolve`.\n\n`formatCheckDigit` is squarely in category 2 by nature \u2014 a ~15-line, well-known standalone algorithm (reverse scan, doubling, digit-sum, mod 10) that is a pure function of a single `String` (`customerCode`) \u2014 yet it's been dropped into category 1 as the only method on the interface carrying real algorithmic loop/branch logic inline. That's the structural inconsistency a maintainer here would flag.\n\n**Suggested change:**\n- Add a small util (e.g. `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.util.LuhnCheckDigit` with a static `compute(String)` / `checkDigit(String)`), mirroring `LocalityResolver`/`TelephoneNormalizer`.\n- Reduce `formatCheckDigit` to the same thin shape as `formatLocality`:\n  ```java\n  @Named(\"formatCheckDigit\")\n  default Integer formatCheckDigit(Owner owner) {\n      if (owner == null || owner.getCustomerCode() == null) {\n          return null;\n      }\n      return LuhnCheckDigit.compute(owner.getCustomerCode());\n  }\n  ```\n\nThis keeps the mapper focused on mapping/field-shaping and puts the reusable algorithm where the codebase already puts reusable algorithms, instead of letting the interface accrete general-purpose computation. The OpenAPI schema addition itself is fine and consistent.",
